<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kamran Shah Durrani</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1842">1842</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Kamran Shah</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kamran Shah <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durrani-empire">Durrani</a> was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sadozai">Sadozai</a> prince, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-shah-durrani">Mahmud Shah Durrani</a>. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barakzai-dynasty">Barakzai</a> brothers took over most of the Durrani Empire around 1818, he and his father held on to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a> as an independent principality. Kamran resisted a long Persian siege in 1837 and 1838, but his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yar-muhammad-khan-alakozai">Yar Muhammad Khan Alakozai</a> became the real ruler and killed him in 1842.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kamran-shah-durrani.jpg" alt="Kamran Shah Durrani" width="320" height="401" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shahzada_Kamran_Durrani.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Kamran Mirza, called Shahzada in Afghan sources, was a grandson of Timur Shah <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durrani-empire">Durrani</a>. While his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-shah-durrani">Mahmud Shah Durrani</a> fought his half-brother Shah Shujah Durrani for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a>, Kamran governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kandahar">Kandahar</a>, and his uncle Firuz al-Din Mirza held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a> under Iranian suzerainty. In 1814 Kamran defeated Firuz al-Din's revolt against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. In April 1818 the minister Fateh Khan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barakzai-dynasty">Barakzai</a> seized Herat; his soldiers plundered the city, and his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dost-mohammad-khan">Dost Mohammad Khan</a> raped Kamran's sister. Kamran lured Fateh Khan to breakfast, had him blinded and imprisoned, and Mahmud Shah later had him executed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghazni">Ghazni</a>, which set off the rising of Fateh Khan's brothers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Driven back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a>, Kamran removed his father from its fortress in 1819 and, after a further conflict in July 1826, defeated him with the backing of Hasan Ali Mirza, the Iranian governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a>. Mahmud Shah withdrew from politics and Kamran became ruler of Herat. When the Iranian crown prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbas-mirza">Abbas Mirza</a> demanded coins and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khutba">Friday prayers</a> in the shah's name, Kamran offered 15,000 toman, and an Iranian army besieged Herat in 1833. The death of Abbas Mirza in November 1833 ended that siege, and a treaty bound Kamran to send a son as hostage and pay tribute.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohammad-shah-qajar">Mohammad Shah Qajar</a>, who came to the throne on 7 November 1834, renewed the demands. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> accused <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a> of harbouring raids into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a>, and Kamran attacked Lash-Juvain and pressed Khaf and Qaen for tribute. On 21 November 1837 an Iranian army of about 30,000 soldiers besieged Herat. The walls held, and the British officer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eldred-pottinger">Eldred Pottinger</a> helped rally the defenders. The historian Jonathan L. Lee judges that his real achievement was persuading Kamran and Yar Muhammad Khan not to surrender. A British naval force occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kharg-island">Kharg Island</a>, and on 9 September 1838 Mohammad Shah lifted the siege.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The siege left the country around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a> devastated, and some inhabitants sold themselves to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmens</a> to keep their families alive. In February 1841 Kamran declared Herat part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a> and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. In 1842 his minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yar-muhammad-khan-alakozai">Yar Muhammad Khan Alakozai</a>, who had been reducing his authority, overthrew and killed him, ending <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durrani-empire">Durrani</a> rule. George William Forrest judged him favourably, but European and Persian records stress his debauchery, and the Afghan historian Sultan Muhammad Khan Khalis saw him as a despot who typified the dynasty's decline.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kamran Mirza Durrani” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>When the Barakzai brothers took over most of the Durrani Empire around 1818, the Sadozai prince Mahmud Shah and his son <b>Kamran Shah</b> held on to Herat, ruling it as an independent principality.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-herat">Principality of Herat</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=34.34,62.2&amp;year=1832" aria-label="Herat on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Kamran Shah Durrani"><image href="/maps/globes/g060n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5156 4628h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5156 4628h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=34.34,62.2&amp;year=1832">What was here in 1832? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
